To design a JSON audit event for a hospital API that reads patient records, we need to consider several aspects of HIPAA compliance and security best practices. Below is a structured approach to creating such an audit event:

### 1. Access Purpose
The access purpose should clearly indicate what the user (in this case, the API) is authorized to do with the patient records.

```json
{
    "auditEvent": {
        "accessPurpose": "Read Patient Records",
        "actor": "API User",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-05T14:30:00Z",
        "resourceReference": "https://api.example.com/patient_records"
    }
}
```

### 2. Actor
The actor should be explicitly defined as who is performing the action on behalf of the user.

```json
{
    "auditEvent": {
        "accessPurpose": "Read Patient Records",
        "actor": "Patient",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-05T14:30:00Z",
        "resourceReference": "https://api.example.com/patient_records"
    }
}
```

### 3. Timestamp
Timestamping ensures that the audit event can be traced back in time if needed. This helps in identifying when the audit occurred.

```json
{
    "auditEvent": {
        "accessPurpose": "Read Patient Records",
        "actor": "Patient",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-05T14:30:00Z",
        "resourceReference": "https://api.example.com/patient_records"
    }
}
```

### 4. Resource Reference
A clear and specific reference to the resource being audited is essential. In this case, it's the `/patient_records` endpoint.

```json
{
    "auditEvent": {
        "accessPurpose": "Read Patient Records",
        "actor": "Patient",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-05T14:30:00Z",
        "resourceReference": "/patient_records"
    }
}
```

### 5. Avoiding Sensitive Data Logging
